Disable biased locking by default to prevent infinite recursion during
class initialization when HotSpot is compiled with clang at -O0.

At -O0, BiasedLocking::revoke_and_rebias() takes a code path that
re-triggers java.lang.Object initialization before _init_state has been
set to being_initialized in instanceKlass::initialize_impl(). Since the
reentrant check depends on _init_state, the recursion is unbounded.

OpenJDK 8 fixed this properly with a separate init_lock (JDK-8028073).
For JDK 7, disabling biased locking is safe and sufficient — it was
removed entirely in JDK 15 (JEP 374) as a net negative on modern
hardware, and the ZERO interpreter already disables it unconditionally.

--- openjdk.orig/hotspot/src/share/vm/runtime/globals.hpp
+++ openjdk/hotspot/src/share/vm/runtime/globals.hpp
@@ -1224,7 +1224,7 @@
   notproduct(bool, PrintCompactFieldsSavings, false,                        \
           "Print how many words were saved with CompactFields")             \
                                                                            \
-  product(bool, UseBiasedLocking, true,                                     \
+  product(bool, UseBiasedLocking, false,                                    \
           "Enable biased locking in JVM")                                   \
                                                                            \
   product(intx, BiasedLockingStartupDelay, 4000,                            \
